# TUI conventions

The UI is a `ratatui` + `crossterm` adapter over the application core. It must
never talk to other adapters directly and never perform network I/O on its
render path.

## Libraries

- `ratatui` for widgets.
- `crossterm` for terminal control and keyboard events.
- `arboard` for clipboard access.
- Optional image rendering (future): `ratatui-image` with sixel or kitty
  protocol. Not MVP.

## Screen model

- Every screen implements a `Screen` trait:

  ```rust
  pub trait Screen {
      fn title(&self) -> &'static str;
      fn render(&self, frame: &mut Frame<'_>, area: Rect);
      fn handle_event(&mut self, evt: &AppEvent) -> Option<Command>;
      fn tick(&mut self, delta: Duration) -> Option<Command>;
  }
  ```

- `Command` is an enum consumed by the dispatcher (`Push`, `Pop`, `Replace`,
  `OpenModal`, `CloseModal`, `Switch`, `Refresh`, `Quit`).
- Screens never mutate the stack directly; they emit commands.

## Navigation stack

- A central `ScreenStack` owns `Vec<Box<dyn Screen>>` and a modal slot.
- `push` / `pop` / `replace` are the only mutators.
- The stack is read by the UI task each frame through an `Arc<RwLock<...>>`
  snapshot or a lock-free double buffer.

## Keybindings

- Bindings are data, not scattered `match` arms.
- A `KeyMap` maps `(ScreenId, KeyEvent)` to an `Action`. Actions are resolved
  first against screen-specific bindings, then against the global map.
- Config overrides the default map. Conflicts are detected on config load and
  rejected with a clear error.

## Layout

- Use `Layout` with `Constraint::Length`, `Constraint::Percentage`,
  `Constraint::Min`. Avoid hard-coded `Rect` math.
- Header, breadcrumb and status bar are rendered by the shell, not by
  individual screens. Screens own the content area only.
- Narrow-terminal fallback: screens with multi-column layouts stack vertically
  when the width is below a threshold defined per screen.

## Data delivery

- Screens subscribe to data via `tokio::sync::mpsc` channels handed to them by
  the dispatcher. They never `await` inside `render` or `handle_event`.
- Long-running tasks are spawned by the dispatcher and tied to the screen's
  lifetime; dropping the screen cancels the task.

## Widgets

- Custom widgets live in `src/adapters/ui/widgets/`.
- Colours are semantic tokens (`Theme::accent`, `Theme::warning`,
  `Theme::success`) resolved against the current palette. No raw RGB in
  widgets.
- Tables use the `ratatui` `Table` widget with row-level highlighting for the
  selected row; column widths are computed from data when possible.

## Input

- Keyboard events are normalised to the application's `Action` enum before
  being dispatched.
- Mouse is out of scope for MVP.

## Rendering budget

- Target 60 fps maximum; more typically 10 fps on idle and up to 30 fps on
  active scroll / animation.
- No screen should need more than one `render` pass per frame.

## Testing

- Screens are tested at the functional level by instantiating them with
  stubbed ports and asserting on `handle_event` outputs.
- The rendered output itself is snapshot-tested with
  `ratatui::backend::TestBackend` for a few representative frames per screen.
